{"title":"Topicwise Test-15 Part Syllabus GATE 2025 EC Analog circuit-3","fileName":"063_Topicwise Test-15 Part Syllabus GATE 2025 EC Analog circuit-3.html","branch":"ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING","series":"GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series Electronics Engineering","provider":"MADE EASY","duration":2700,"questions":[{"qnum":1,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>\n Consider the circuit shown below:</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/4a6530f4458ef85ad3980c08d52df060d515b637.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n The basic op-amp shown in the circuit is ideal. The mathematical operation performed by the amplifier circuit and the value of <em>V<sub>o</sub></em> if <img src=\"/media/2fee2b370cdaedca1a4da3f02d0a5220cf395e1e.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> is respectively given by</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Summation; 60 V</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> Integrator ; 20 V</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> Subtraction ; 30 V</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> Logarithm; 20 V</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (c)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/ed48949225dea0d5be73d46597c345ae7678d307.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":2,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>\n For the circuit shown, <em>V</em><sub>out</sub> for the input <em>V</em><sub>in</sub> is –12 V. Then, the value of <em>V</em><sub>in</sub> may be</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/8e599a414cada8ed4f8689de05c48014e0fb375f.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> –5 V</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 2 V</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> –0.5 V</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 0.5 V</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (a)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/ad4c9afb09203b1c7024f6b3b9e0186b864cafab.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":3,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>\n For an ideal op-amp, which of the following properties is not correct?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Open loop voltage gain is infinite</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> Input resistance is infinite</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> Slew rate is infinite</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> CMRR is zero</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (d)<br />\n For ideal op-amp, CMRR is infinite. i.e. differential gain is infinite and common-mode gain is zero</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":4,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>\n For the circuit given below,</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/1784116f90cc593bdffd5ce14701ba4725a7b657.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n If <em>X</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>X</em><sub>2</sub> are inductors and <em>X</em><sub>3</sub> is capacitor, then the circuit is know as</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Colpitts oscillator</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> Wien bridge oscillator</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> Hartley oscillator</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> None of the these</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (c)<br />\n In Hartley Oscillator, <em>X</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>X</em><sub>2</sub> are inductors and <em>X</em><sub>3</sub> is a capacitor and in the Colpitts oscillator, <em>X</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>X</em><sub>2</sub> are capacitors and <em>X</em><sub>3</sub> is an inductor.</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":5,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>\n Consider the Schmitt trigger with parameters <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> = 10 kΩ and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> = 90 kΩ. Let <em>V<sub>H</sub></em> = 10 V and <em>V<sub>L</sub></em> = –10 V.</p>\n<p>\n The hysteresis width of the Schmitt trigger shown below is ______.</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/ff9030b65030d3ed08b489b7868a2a3f794717ee.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 0.1 V</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 0 V</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 2 V</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 1 V</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (c)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/749d684a4c4ad741a181175c90aaa67680ca6f43.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":6,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n For an input of <img src=\"/media/5a20453c6595762c5b6f7f60acc0cab00a09308a.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> mV in the circuit shown, the value of maximum frequency that may be used if the op-amp has the slew rate SR = 0.4 V/μs is _______ kHz. (Rounded off to two decimal places)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/7afd680cec9c11b7a636312a187a7dc8206465cd.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"0.28","high":"0.34"},"solution":"<p>\n 2 (0.28 - 0.34)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/10c9113cedb2f50ad3dbd04c699a2778ef8669dd.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":7,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n Consider the integrator circuit shown in figure,</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/554f76c0037511d2da819998abd61ca9c8029622.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n Assume that voltage <em>V<sub>C</sub></em> across the capacitor is zero at t = 0. A step input voltage of <em>V</em><sub>1</sub> = –1V is applied at <em>t</em> = 0. The time constant required such that the output reaches +10 V at <em>t</em> = 1 ms is _______ msec.</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"0.08","high":"0.12"},"solution":"<p>\n 0.1 (0.08 - 0.12)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/ff16dd08f6f703a10d58de0faf8bd9ae468d2829.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":8,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n Consider an ideal summing amplifier as shown in the figure below:</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/15badf8d444eefb07724c026db99100d75a774a5.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n If <img src=\"/media/54b4137bc79fd410e187f0fd379b488ca71c25e1.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> <em>R<sub>f</sub></em> = 40 kΩ and <em>V<sub>o</sub></em> = –1.5 mV for <em>V</em><sub>1</sub> = 250 μV, <em>V</em><sub>2</sub> = 200 μV and <em>V</em><sub>3</sub> = 75 μV.<br />\n Then, the resistance <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> is ____________ kΩ.</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"20","high":"20"},"solution":"<p>\n 20 (20 - 20)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/d3302e6a19176332ea38b179cc382512db9dece4.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/45aec7dd04cb9460d3ac441d0613cacaca216db7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":9,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,C,D","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n Consider the circuit given below,</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/1ec8fbe6be6ebbf6d145cd9d2389d4930f6d75f2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n If <img src=\"/media/06ab25f5efabe1d9b5d28b4baf69de4bd8e7c717.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> then which of the following option(s) is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <p>\n Load current <img src=\"/media/061dea710ff6293623678b5320e4756b6fa20f88.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> is independent of <em>Z<sub>L</sub></em> and is equal to 5 mA.</p>\n</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <p>\n Load current <img src=\"/media/5b54f542af465a51ab867eeaa43670e81c8a7040.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> is dependent on <em>Z<sub>L</sub></em> and is equal to 0.5 mA.</p>\n</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <p>\n The output voltage <em>V</em><sub>o</sub> = 6 V.</p>\n</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <p>\n Current i3 = 5.5 mA</p>\n</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (a, c, d)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/1598a0c06c603c1d27f1d08c79b8e225559b95bd.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/04472638e9e217f01ca16b2506cd7b669808d1f0.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":10,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>\n A crystal has the following parameters: <em>L<sub>S</sub></em> = 0.33 H, <em>C<sub>S</sub></em> = 0.065 pF, <em>C<sub>P</sub></em> = 1 pF and <em>R<sub>S</sub></em> = 5.5 kΩ. By what percent does the parallel resonant frequency exceed the series resonant frequency?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 5%</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 9%</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 3.2%</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 6%</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (c)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/afe9f3e63ee752d0a82b352f0b45d7de637876c2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":11,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>\n An amplifier with feedback has voltage gain equal to 40. To produce a specified output, the input voltage required without feedback is 0.1 V. When feedback has been provided the input must be increased to 2.4 V to produce the same output. The value of feedback ratio is</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> –1.5</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> –0.99</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> –1</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> –0.024</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (d)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/00a94c75b5980176e5ba98c865ec3cb0535e2033.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/778541734d882d07c3b7746f965fa3189583b6cf.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":12,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>\n An amplifier gain changes by ±10%. Using negative feedback, the amplifier is to be modified to provide a gain of 100 with ±0.1% variation. The required open loop gain of the amplifier is</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <p>\n 10<sup>5</sup></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <p>\n 10<sup>6</sup></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <p>\n 10<sup>4</sup></p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <p>\n 10<sup>3</sup></p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (c)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/b9d5603fa5c815444eb9b080165421bda94cbee0.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":13,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>\n An amplifier has an input impedance of 1 kΩ, output impedance of 10 kΩ and a voltage gain of 10<sup>4</sup>. If a voltage series negative feedback with β = 0.02 is applied to it, then the input and output impedance of the amplifier is given by</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/8622835e941c9754238f14ab543b6f186f641094.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/d2ed9bc2ae257b12ae617b31b898e8e59e38739c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/7ffcfd7f6585d2a60cebf5b44a67ab730032c9f9.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/d75315164ab57aade4c59d23a892e0d20e8b7eb7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (b)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/6cca290b68bb82273eeafbccdb9401f1c8a4a004.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/eba2c3f071cbaf85b69f141dc52708dad7c720ee.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":14,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n For the circuit shown below, the frequency of oscillation is _____ Hertz.<br />\n (Rounded off to two decimal places) (Assume op-amp to be ideal)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/5962e896fe7dbb1b8b9eb054780df55526e6c057.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"33.8","high":"33.9"},"solution":"<p>\n 33.86 (33.80 - 33.90)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/1cc2d9be6240161aa1afa5122fa525fba6a3fa0d.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/27dc2488fcd595a4ce935e6f9ecdf00c50e4ec4f.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":15,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n For the circuit shown below, the output voltage in steady state condition, is (i) <em>V</em><sub><em>o</em>1</sub> when switch S is open, and (ii) <em>V</em><sub><em>o</em>2</sub> when switch S is closed. The value of <em>V</em><sub><em>o</em>1</sub> +<em>V</em><sub><em>o</em>2</sub> is _______ Volts.</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/ed5ffb21810084433b06261ad155b8060f06ecb2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"-3","high":"-3"},"solution":"<p>\n -3 (-3 - -3)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/cb9c346d9c219500e69fcba7c6b95762701d6437.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":16,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n An amplifier has a differential gain of <em>A<sub>d</sub></em> = 6000 and the input voltage of <img src=\"/media/b54a8564288d64eb7504a9687f1c852a7d379e59.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> μV and <img src=\"/media/e4edd8552b1ce52c98ace9d6041bfd85f6704cf8.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> The difference between the output for CMRR of 200 and 10<sup>5</sup> is ________ mV. (Rounded off to one decimal place)</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"4","high":"6"},"solution":"<p>\n 5.1 (4 - 6)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/777b15a9195536a8b115ceb50ec8662038d3b3af.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/08ae34ff71f34f9d111a73855f447a001f30afe2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":17,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,B,C","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>\n Consider the circuit shown below,</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/940520d0bc9113aa7253ce2f076e9dbf1b397420.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<p>\n Which of the following option(s) is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/2547c0fde88dc341985c4520b552999295d88584.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/51e79d3b14a8b1096e4f4f022b0d33fbc7f8aa17.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> The difference between upper and lower cut-off frequency is 80 Hz.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <p>\n <img src=\"/media/1c04f057b670ca5f69bb88c72e1a01a1185360ec.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>\n (a, b, c)</p>\n<p>\n <img src=\"/media/eaf2099c813c2428c9d96656331d2c98b55ecd19.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>","subject":"Technical"}],"questionCount":17,"parsedAt":"2026-11-10T19:39:15.772Z"}